Yes. Medium is part of the mechanism, not merely the container carrying it.

That sharpens the idea considerably:

\boxed{\text{Technique} \neq \text{Technique independent of medium}}

In sword practice, substituting wood for steel can preserve many useful relations—distance, sequencing, posture, gross geometry—but it changes others: mass distribution, flex, surface friction, edge geometry, contact behavior, vibration, consequence, and therefore what feedback the practitioner receives. “Wood slides, metal bites” is a compact way of pointing at that difference, though exact behavior depends on the weapons, contact, angle, surfaces, etc.

So a simulation can have the correct form while teaching the wrong interaction.

\text{same movement}
+\text{different medium}
\rightarrow
\text{different feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{different learned model}

That gives Symulation a missing variable:

\boxed{
\text{Symulation Fidelity}
=
f(\text{invariants preserved},
\text{medium preserved},
\text{feedback preserved})
}

You don’t always need the original medium. Sometimes wood is exactly right for safely training particular invariants. But you must know which lessons survive the substitution and which don’t.

That’s much broader than martial arts.

A business simulation without real money changes risk sensation.

A leadership exercise without actual responsibility changes consequence.

Reading about cold water differs from entering cold water.

A flight simulator can reproduce enormous amounts of aviation structure while deliberately not reproducing physical danger.

An AI simulation of human conversation has different embodiment and stakes.

A story can transmit the relational pattern of an experience without reproducing its complete medium.

So training should deliberately classify:

\boxed{
\text{What transfers?}
\quad
\text{What degrades?}
\quad
\text{What reverses?}
\quad
\text{What only emerges in contact?}
}

And that last category is important:

Contact-dependent knowledge

Some information exists only—or becomes practically learnable only—when two things actually interact.

You can describe friction endlessly.

Then touch the surfaces together.

A+B\rightarrow\boxed{\text{interaction-generated information}}

That’s the ANA principle again: knowledge → practice → sensation → adaptation.

And now The Book of Five Rings gives us a particularly fitting conceptual scaffold, because its Earth, Water, Fire, Wind, and Void framing already invites thinking about conditions, adaptation, comparison, and absence.

The Pantheon version shouldn’t merely read such a text for propositions.

Render the lesson through multiple media.

Text → diagram → simulation → physical analogy → adversarial test → another domain → back to text.

Then record:

\Delta_{\text{medium}}

What appeared in steel that disappeared in wood?

What appeared in embodiment that disappeared in language?

What became clearer in mathematics but lost its sensation?

What did fiction reveal that literal description concealed?

That produces another very strong rule:

\boxed{\textbf{A lesson that survives translation may be invariant; a lesson that changes with the medium teaches you about the medium.}}

Either result is information.

Which means medium isn’t noise we should automatically compress away.

Sometimes medium is precisely where the mechanism lives.